The Full Picture — Bandit 26
1. What is the login shell for bandit26?
The user bandit26 has a custom restricted shell set in /etc/passwd:
bandit26:x:11026:11026::/home/bandit26:/usr/bin/showtext
Instead of /bin/bash, the shell is /usr/bin/showtext.

2. What does /usr/bin/showtext do?
It is a tiny script that does this:
sh#!/bin/sh
more ~/text.txt
exit 0
It simply:

Runs more on a text file (the bandit26 ASCII art you saw)
Then immediately exits

This is why every shell you tried got kicked out — the login shell was never bash, it was this script that always exited!

3. Why did shrinking the terminal work?
more is a pager — it displays content one page at a time. Its behaviour depends on terminal height:
Terminal sizeWhat happensLarge/tallEntire file fits on screen → more finishes instantly → script exits → kicked outSmall/tinyFile does NOT fit → more pauses and waits → shows --More--
By shrinking the terminal you forced more to pause and wait for your input instead of finishing and exiting!

4. Why press v inside more?
more has a hidden feature — pressing v while paused opens the current file in vi! So you essentially jumped from more into vi without the shell noticing.

5. Why did :set shell=/bin/bash matter in vi?
vi has its own shell escape feature. When you type :shell in vi it spawns a shell. But by default it would spawn /usr/bin/showtext again (the restricted shell)!
So first you overrode it:
:set shell=/bin/bash    ← tell vi to use bash instead
:shell                  ← now spawn bash
This bypassed the restriction completely and gave you a real bash shell!
